-- title:
--   One stationary law per allocation class of the static chain
-- statement:
--   Let $q^s$ be feasible for Problem (2), and let $(K_c)_c$ be an allocation of the $K$ vehicles to the networks of $q^s$, $\sum_c K_c=K$. Then the chain of the static policy $q^s$ has **exactly one** stationary distribution concentrated on the states with allocation $(K_c)$.
--
--   This is the paper's statement (p. 6) that under the static policy "the network is irreducible and belongs to the class of BCMP networks", in the multi-network setting of §6, where each network keeps its vehicles. It provides the steady state $\pi$ with respect to which availabilities $\alpha_i$ and the reward $R(q^s,K^*)$ are computed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - reading: the network is irreducible and belongs to the class of BCMP networks (p. 6) → for every allocation of the vehicles to the networks of q^s, exactly one stationary law of the static chain is concentrated on that allocation class; reason: irreducibility (on the recurrent states) is what makes the steady state unique; with several networks each keeps its vehicles, so it holds per allocation class; queues without flow are transient and empty on the class
--   - generalization: q^s optimal for Problem (2) with Δ ≥ K → q^s feasible for Problem (2), any Δ; reason: only the bounds 0 ≤ q^s ≤ 1 and balanced flows are used; an optimal q^s is feasible
--   - generalization: q^s induces one strongly connected network (p. 6; Section 3) → q^s induces any number of networks; the statement is per network; reason: Section 6 relaxes the assumption; a single network is the case of one component
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → no connectivity assumption on the demand graph; reason: no step uses it; strongly connected instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, p. 6 (unnumbered); §6, p. 17

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsFeasible2
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_staticPolicy
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_ConcentratedOn

namespace VehShare.MultiNet

/-- **One stationary law per allocation class under the static policy.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), p. 6 (unnumbered): "Then the network is irreducible and belongs to the class of BCMP networks
(Baskett et al. (1975))"; §6, p. 17, for several networks.

Let `q^s` be feasible for Problem (2). For every allocation `(K_c)_c` of the `K` vehicles to the
networks of `q^s` (`∑_c K_c = K`), the chain of the static policy has exactly one stationary
distribution concentrated on the states with that allocation.

**Formalization Note.** Only feasibility of `q^s` (bounds and balanced flows) is assumed, with any
`Δ` (a generalization of the paper's optimal `q^s`). -/
theorem static_stationary_unique (P : Instance) (K : ℕ) (Δ : ℝ) (qs : P.L → P.L → ℝ)
    (hqs : IsFeasible2 P Δ qs) (Kc : (flowGraph P qs).ConnectedComponent → ℕ)
    (hKc : ∑ c, Kc c = K) :
    ∃! π : State P K → ℝ,
      IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1) π ∧ ConcentratedOn P qs Kc π := by sorry

end VehShare.MultiNet
